  "notes": "The three scales pulled hardest apart on: eyes/eye (very high picturability and violence, but near-zero fatality - blood from the eyes is vivid and disturbing but not lethal); veins, wrists, and temples (elevated fatality from real vascular/cranial anatomy despite only moderate picturability, since the danger is anatomical knowledge rather than a clear visual); body (near-zero picturability, since it names no location at all, alongside moderate fatality and violence from the sense of extensive, generalized bleeding); and fingers (low fatality but high violence, since bleeding/broken fingers read as torture rather than as a mortal wound). The generic injury nouns wound/wounds/cut/cuts score low on picturability by construction, since they name the injury rather than a body-part location, while still carrying moderate violence and fatality. Two NOT_SCORABLE calls were judgment rather than mechanical: 'victim' is grammatical (\"blood poured from his victim\") but names a person, not an injury site; 'mouth．' carries a trailing full-width period that makes it a distinct, likely-corrupted token rather than clean 'mouth', so it was not scored as equivalent to 'mouth'."
